WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:06.200
Speaker 1: So Republicans have blocked even
debating and discussing this major voting

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.910
rights bill that we've been talking about
now for quite some time, and we are really

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:17.440
now getting into sort of the most disgusting
and destructive of American politics.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:24.940
And, of course, huge blame goes directly to
the Republicans who voted against even debating

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:26.810
the voting rights bill.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:34.210
But also, we can't ignore the fact that the
great negotiation that Joe Biden has claimed

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:41.149
he would be able to do has not even led to
a debate about the voting rights bill.

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:45.629
And we're going to have video from the floor
of the Senate and and much more.

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:48.800
And in fact, maybe we'll even start there.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:57.039
This is the moment where Kamala Harris, who
presided over the Senate during this vote,

00:00:57.039 --> 00:01:02.440
announced that the bill was it wasn't we were
not moving forward into debate.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:03.440
Take a look at this.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:09.159
Speaker 2: Yays are 50, the nays are fifty
three fifths of the senators duly chosen and

00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:15.389
sworn not having voted in the affirmative,
the motion is not agreed to.

00:01:15.389 --> 00:01:22.279
Speaker 1: Now, what you have to understand
when you evaluate this is that there really

00:01:22.279 --> 00:01:26.439
are two completely different realities on
this issue.

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:32.069
Mitch McConnell, for example, came out and
said we don't even really have a voting rights

00:01:32.069 --> 00:01:33.950
issue in this country.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:39.469
There aren't even people trying to suppress
the ability of individuals to vote.

00:01:39.469 --> 00:01:44.079
He said this, I guess with for Mitch McConnell
counts as a straight face.

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:45.950
Let's take a look at what Mitch McConnell
had to say.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.249
I mean, how do you say this with a straight
face?

00:01:48.249 --> 00:01:55.189
How do you have the gumption and I mean the
tolerance for cringe to come out and say the

00:01:55.189 --> 00:01:56.189
following?

00:01:56.189 --> 00:02:00.549
Speaker 3: And many of you have written about
the subject of big lies.

00:02:00.549 --> 00:02:08.269
The biggest lie being told in American politics
in recent weeks has been that the states are

00:02:08.269 --> 00:02:12.440
involved in a systematic effort to suppress
the vote.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.069
Speaker 1: That's just a wash.

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:16.790
Why would anyone think that that's going on?

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:21.290
I mean, other than the fact that Republicans
have been openly admitting it since at least

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:27.569
2012 when Mike Turzai said voter ID is going
to make it so that Mitt Romney wins Pennsylvania

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:31.490
and then everything else that happened in
the nine years that followed and the two hundred

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:35.240
and fifty something bills across the country,
Speaker 3: those arguments are being made

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:40.390
by people who obviously haven't read any of
these new state laws because that's not happening.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:46.640
As we all know, we had the biggest turnout
last year since nineteen hundred.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.870
Speaker 1: Now, that's a red herring.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.689
That's a complete and total, very biggest
turnout since 1900.

00:02:51.689 --> 00:02:54.299
How many people lived in the United States
in 1900?

00:02:54.299 --> 00:02:59.439
So there's three or four different reasons
why that's not a good argument.

00:02:59.439 --> 00:03:05.219
Number one, as the population continues to
get larger and larger, you would expect more

00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:06.530
and more people to vote.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:08.970
Trump often points to, hey, I got 75 million
votes.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:14.590
He really got 74 that you know, that that
makes it hard to believe that I lost, given

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:16.430
that 74 million people voted for me.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:19.099
Well, even more people voted for Joe Biden.

00:03:19.099 --> 00:03:23.540
And in addition to that, we don't know how
many people wanted to vote.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.650
So it's just a complete and total red herring.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.219
Let's just look at a little bit more of this.

00:03:27.219 --> 00:03:35.550
Speaker 3: There is no effort in any state
in America to suppress votes based upon.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:39.180
Suppression of minority participation.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:42.060
Speaker 1: So that's a complete and total
lie.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:46.220
I mean, it couldn't be it couldn't be a more
dishonest thing to say.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:49.900
So let's look at a little bit of the reporting
about this CNN reporting.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:55.200
Senate Republicans blocked signature Democratic
election bill in key test vote, saying that

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.700
Senate Democrats suffered a loss when Republican
opposition sunk their signature voting an

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:04.889
election bill during a key test vote, underscoring
the limits of the party's power with the narrowest

00:04:04.889 --> 00:04:06.159
possible Senate majority.

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:13.690
A procedural vote to open debate was defeated
by 50 50, falling short of the 60 votes needed.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:18.359
Democrats were united in favor after securing
Joe Manchin support, but Republicans were

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:22.570
united against it, causing the measure to
fail to advance.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:26.039
Democratic senators have pitched the legislation
as a necessary counter to state level efforts

00:04:26.039 --> 00:04:28.130
to restrict voting access.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:33.750
Republicans have decried it as a partizan
power grab and a federal overreach into state

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.400
voting and election systems.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.669
The article goes on to say, Senate Democrats
are already beginning to map out their next

00:04:40.669 --> 00:04:45.550
steps to draw attention to the issue and put
the focus on a critical battleground state,

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:46.550
Georgia.

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:53.130
Now, one of the lies that they are already
telling about Georgia is, hey, listen, voter

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:55.069
purges are normal.

00:04:55.069 --> 00:05:03.940
This is one of the techniques or tactics that
is being used by Republicans to hurt Democrats

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:04.970
in elections.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:10.690
And it is to purge voter rolls, but to say
every state does it.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:17.710
Now, it's true that every state does it, but
with far less restrictive barometer's than

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:20.169
what Georgia is putting in place.

00:05:20.169 --> 00:05:22.960
And so you really have to look at the details.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:28.639
And this is one of those issues where if you're
not following it relatively closely, you're

00:05:28.639 --> 00:05:34.410
not going to understand why the bullet points
from Republicans fall short.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.170
And this everybody does it.

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:43.349
One is is really a an important example, an
illuminating example, which is they insist

00:05:43.349 --> 00:05:49.729
every state has a system for deciding who
should no longer be considered an active registered

00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:50.729
voter.

00:05:50.729 --> 00:05:56.500
And it depends on how recently you have voted
and other factors.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:01.780
What's being done in Georgia is far more aggressive
and it's being done in a very tailored way

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:07.319
that while in an overt way it doesn't target
minority voters, it's focused on those more

00:06:07.319 --> 00:06:13.970
likely to be voting for Democrats who happen
to disproportionately be nonwhite voters.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:15.039
So I get it.

00:06:15.039 --> 00:06:17.530
People are busy, people are working, people
have lives.

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:22.159
So unless you're following it closely, you
would not necessarily understand why the claims

00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:23.419
that are being made are so deceptive.

00:06:23.419 --> 00:06:29.379
Now, I want to talk about one other thing
here, which is the filibuster aspect of this.

00:06:29.379 --> 00:06:34.650
There is a good article in the L.A. Times,
an editorial by the Times editorial board

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:38.259
which says Senate Republicans won't even consider
voting rights.

00:06:38.259 --> 00:06:40.270
The filibuster must go.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:45.639
And it makes sense that there is increased
pressure to do away with or modify the filibuster

00:06:45.639 --> 00:06:48.159
because it's not even that they're voting
against the bill.

00:06:48.159 --> 00:06:49.650
They're voting against debating the bill.

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:53.539
And you need 60 votes in order to move forward,
they wrote yesterday.

00:06:53.539 --> 00:06:57.909
As expected, debate on a bill to protect voting
rights was blocked in the Senate when Democratic

00:06:57.909 --> 00:07:01.009
sponsors failed to garner 60 votes.

00:07:01.009 --> 00:07:06.220
But in reaching out to centrist Joe Manchin,
Democrats indicated they were open to compromise.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:09.730
The shameful reaction of Senate Republicans,
on the other hand, demonstrated that they

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:15.500
are willing to ignore laws approved by Republican
controlled legislatures that make voting harder.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.960
Those measures reflect the lingering malign
influence of Donald Trump's big lie that the

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.800
2020 election was stolen.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:28.530
They go on to say about the filibuster, and
I want to find the exact point where that

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:30.780
is.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:37.300
After Tuesday's votes, Schumer said that it
represented the starting gun, not the finish

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:38.300
line.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:43.139
Manchin and other and another Democratic senator,
Kirsten Sinema of Arizona, have resisted the

00:07:43.139 --> 00:07:45.099
idea of abolishing the filibuster.

00:07:45.099 --> 00:07:49.530
But if Republican senators continue to stonewall
reasonable legislation to protect the right

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:55.050
to vote, those two reluctant Democrats must
be pressed to recognize that at least we're

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:57.300
protecting democracy is concerned.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.050
The filibuster must go.

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:01.050
And this is absolutely and totally correct.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:07.620
Now, whether the filibuster must go in total,
which potentially is going to have repercussions

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:14.949
against Democrats when Republicans invariably
take control again, at least in practical

00:08:14.949 --> 00:08:19.520
terms, you either need to reform it to require
a talking filibuster.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:23.830
You either need to require the 40 no votes
to be in the Senate at all times in order

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:26.289
to continue with something absolutely has
to be done.

00:08:26.289 --> 00:08:33.500
I think at this point it's been clear, you
know, if Republicans and Democrats were equal

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:35.440
in their willingness.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:40.490
To do whatever needs to be done to move a
bill forward, then maybe we wouldn't be having

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:41.710
this conversation.

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:48.730
But Democrats have shown that they are not
willing to do what needs to be done in the

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:55.160
way that Republicans are to get moving forward
on legislation that they claim is important

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:56.160
to them.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.280
So something is going to have to be done to
the filibuster.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:08:59.450
It absolutely is.

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:06.470
Time and again, if Republicans said we believe
we have a really strong case, why this bill

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:09.250
should not pass and we plan not to support
it.

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:13.870
But let's debate the bill and then make their
case against the bill during the debate, I

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:18.590
would have a different perspective, but they're
saying no to even debating the bill.

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:31.590
Something has to be done and the filibuster
is going to have to be a big piece 

00:09:31.590 --> 00:10:28.520
of that.

